The jadx command is likely hanging due to insufficient Java heap memory or decompiling a very large multi-dex APK, which takes significant time after the INFO - loading ... stage. Notice also the typo in your output folder name (wolt_decomipled), though that does not stop the process.
Large commercial apps like Wolt contain tens of thousands of classes and heavy obfuscation, causing jadx to stall or silently run out of memory on laptops like a Dell Latitude 3380.

Quick Fixes to Try

• Increase Java memory allocation: jadx defaults to a low RAM limit. Run it with explicit heap memory flags so the JVM does not freeze while parsing large DEX files:bash
JAVA_OPTS="-Xmx4G" jadx -d wolt_decompiled Wolt.apk
Use code with caution.
• Skip resources to speed it up: Decoding XML layouts and assets often causes hangs. If you only need the Java/Kotlin source code, use the -r flag:bash
JAVA_OPTS="-Xmx4G" jadx -r -d wolt_decompiled Wolt.apk
Use code with caution.
• Limit thread count: On dual-core or older mobile CPUs, too many threads cause severe thrashing. Limit the jobs with -j:bash
JAVA_OPTS="-Xmx4G" jadx -j 2 -r -d wolt_decompiled Wolt.apk
Use code with caution.
• Verify the APK integrity: Make sure Wolt.apk is not a split APK wrapper or a corrupted download. Run file Wolt.apk or unzip -l Wolt.apk | head to confirm it is a valid archive containing classes.dex.

Checking if It Is Frozen or Working

• Monitor CPU and RAM: Open a second terminal and run htop or top. If java is using high CPU, jadx is still actively working and just needs more time.
• Enable verbose logging: Add -v (or --log-level DEBUG) to see the exact class or resource where jadx gets stuck.
